@page "/my-deliveries"
@* FR-25, FR-27, FR-96: what a driver is carrying, and what a client asked for.

   Neither role edits or deletes a delivery, so there is no edit dialog and no delete prompt: both
   are dispatch's. Opening one is split rather than absent. A client asks for a delivery of their own
   (FR-89 to FR-91) through the request dialog below, which is why this screen carries a form at all;
   a driver asks for nothing, so neither the action nor the dialog is rendered for them. Withheld,
   not decided - IAccessGuard.RequireDeliveryComposer refuses a driver's request whether or not this
   screen ever drew a button (FR-12).

   The request form is deliberately not the dispatch board's. That one names a driver, a client and a
   delivery window; this one cannot, because RequestDeliveryCommand has no field for any of them.

   What both roles do have is the delivery's history. A driver advances the parcel they are carrying
   (FR-26) and a client adds a note to their own delivery (FR-107), so the row's history action opens
   the shared timeline panel - with the transition buttons drawn for a driver and withheld from a
   client (FR-90). Withheld, not decided: IAccessGuard.RequireAssignedDriver refuses a client's
   status change whether or not this screen ever drew a button (FR-12).

   The rows are AssignedDeliverySummary, which has no driver and no client field at all. That is
   what makes FR-27 and FR-96 structural: a counterparty's name cannot reach this page through a
   mapping mistake, because there is nowhere on the type to put one (AD-17).

   The Roles argument routes a wrong role to /access-denied (FR-79). It is a convenience, not the
   decision: IAccessGuard.RequireScope inside IDeliveryService is what narrows the rows, and it runs
   whether or not this screen was ever rendered. *@
@attribute [Authorize(Roles = "Driver,Client")]
@* Named for what it answers rather than for the capability: a member called `Deliveries` here would
   shadow the dispatch screen's own component type in this namespace. *@
@inject IDeliveryService Mine
@inject ICurrentUser Caller
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@* The dispatch board's address box, reused rather than copied. It is framework-free and takes its
   search delegate as a parameter precisely so a second screen can drive it, and FR-104's rule about
   which box fills which point is one rule however many forms ask it. Aliased because `Deliveries`
   alone reads as two things in this file - the component type and the Application namespace. *@
@using PlaceSearch = DriveTrack.Web.Components.Pages.Deliveries.PlaceSearch
@inherits CircuitScopedComponent

<PageTitle>@Localizer["MyDeliveriesTitle"]</PageTitle>

<h1>
    <Icon Name="IconName.Deliveries" />@Localizer["MyDeliveriesTitle"]
</h1>

@* FR-89: the client's one way of asking for a delivery, on the one screen their deliveries live on.
   A driver never sees it - OffersRequest says so - and the guard refuses them either way. *@
@if (_offersRequest)
{
    <p>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary dt-delivery-request" @onclick="OpenRequestAsync">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Create" />@Localizer["RequestDeliveryAction"]
        </button>
    </p>
}

@if (IsTruncated)
{
    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="status">@Localizer["DeliveriesTruncated"]</div>
}

<DtDataTable TItem="AssignedDeliverySummary"
             Items="_deliveries"
             IsLoading="_loading"
             EmptyText="@Localizer["MyDeliveriesEmpty"]">
    <HeaderTemplate>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["Pickup"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["Dropoff"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["PackageDetails"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["PackageWeightKg"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["DeliveryNotes"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["WindowLatestAt"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["Status"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["Overdue"]</th>
        <th scope="col">@Localizer["Actions"]</th>
    </HeaderTemplate>
    <RowTemplate Context="delivery">
        @* FR-94: the address when one has been resolved, the coordinates when none has. Nothing on
           this screen resolves one - that is story 5.2's port - and an empty cell would be the
           whole location missing rather than only its name. *@
        @* FR-21: the cell is the way to the map here for the same reason it is on the dispatch
           board. The requirement names no role, and a driver reading an address as text is the
           one person on the system who most needs to see where it is. The label is the resolved
           address, or the raw coordinates when none resolved (FR-94), so the cell is never
           empty. *@
        <td>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm dt-my-delivery-pickup"
                    @onclick="ShowLocationHandler(delivery.Pickup)">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Location" />@Describe(delivery.Pickup)
            </button>
        </td>
        <td>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm dt-my-delivery-dropoff"
                    @onclick="ShowLocationHandler(delivery.Dropoff)">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Location" />@Describe(delivery.Dropoff)
            </button>
        </td>
        <td>@delivery.PackageDetails</td>
        <td>@delivery.PackageWeightKg</td>
        <td>@delivery.DeliveryNotes</td>
        <td>@Window(delivery)</td>
        @* The same status vocabulary the dispatch board renders. One component instead of a second
           copy of four localizer keys, which is the copy that goes stale. *@
        <td><DeliveryStatusLabel Status="delivery.Status" /></td>
        <td>
            @if (delivery.IsOverdue)
            {
                <span class="badge text-bg-danger">@Localizer["Overdue"]</span>
            }
        </td>
        <td>
            @* FR-107 and FR-108 for both roles, FR-26 for the driver: a client reads the history
               and adds a note, a driver does that and advances the parcel they are carrying. *@
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm dt-delivery-timeline"
                    @onclick="TimelineHandler(delivery)">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Timeline" />@Localizer["Timeline"]
            </button>

            @* FR-119: the capture, offered to the driver carrying the parcel and withheld from the
               client who asked for it. Withheld, not decided - IAccessGuard.RequireAssignedDriver
               refuses a client's capture whether or not this screen ever drew a button (FR-12). *@
            @if (OffersProofCapture(_role, _captured.Contains(delivery.Id)))
            {
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm dt-proof-capture"
                        @onclick="CaptureHandler(delivery)">
                    <Icon Name="IconName.Camera" />@Localizer["ProofCapture"]
                </button>
            }

            @* FR-122: reading the evidence is for anyone who can see the delivery, the client
               included. The panel answers "nothing captured yet" the same way it answers "not
               yours", which is what stops the action disclosing anything by being pressed. *@
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm dt-proof-open"
                    @onclick="ProofHandler(delivery)">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Proof" />@Localizer["ProofOpen"]
            </button>
        </td>
    </RowTemplate>
</DtDataTable>

@* FR-21: the same map component the dispatch board opens, with picking off. One map, two jobs
   (NFR-26). No OnClosed: the viewed point is the only state it holds and the next open overwrites
   it, so there is nothing stale for a close to drop. *@
<DtDialog @ref="_mapDialog" Title="@Localizer["LocationTitle"]">
    <ChildContent>
        @if (_viewed is not null)
        {
            <DtMap Value="_viewed" Picker="false" />
        }
    </ChildContent>
</DtDialog>

@* Built only once a row has been chosen, so the screen costs no timeline read until somebody asks
   for one - and dropped again on close, so each open builds a new panel rather than reusing one
   holding the status the delivery had when it was last opened. *@
<DtDialog @ref="_timelineDialog" Title="@Localizer["TimelineTitle"]" OnClosed="CloseTimeline">
    <ChildContent>
        @if (_timeline is not null)
        {
            @* The flag draws or hides the transition buttons; it authorizes nothing. A client who
               reached this markup another way is still refused by IAccessGuard, and a driver is
               still refused on a delivery that is not theirs (FR-12, FR-26). *@
            <DeliveryTimeline DeliveryId="_timeline.Id"
                              Status="_timeline.Status"
                              AllowStatusChange="_offersStatusChange"
                              OnChanged="LoadAsync" />
        }
    </ChildContent>
</DtDialog>

@* Built only once a row has been chosen, for the reason the timeline panel is: the screen costs no
   proof read until somebody asks for one, and each open builds a new panel rather than reusing one
   holding whatever the last row had. *@
<DtDialog @ref="_captureDialog" Title="@Localizer["ProofCaptureTitle"]" OnClosed="CloseCapture">
    <ChildContent>
        @if (_capturing is not null)
        {
            <ProofCapture DeliveryId="_capturing.Id" OnCaptured="CapturedAsync" />
        }
    </ChildContent>
</DtDialog>

<DtDialog @ref="_proofDialog" Title="@Localizer["ProofViewTitle"]" OnClosed="CloseProof">
    <ChildContent>
        @if (_viewing is not null)
        {
            <ProofView DeliveryId="_viewing.Id" />
        }
    </ChildContent>
</DtDialog>

@* FR-89 to FR-91. Rendered at all only for a client, so a driver's page carries no request markup
   to find rather than a hidden dialog they cannot open - which is the difference between "the
   affordance is withheld" and "the affordance is styled away".

   The shape is the dispatch board's create dialog and the differences are the story: no driver box,
   no client box and no window bounds, because RequestDeliveryCommand has no field to bind them to.
   The two address blocks sit OUTSIDE <EditForm> for the reason they do there - an <input> inside a
   form whose default button submits would make Enter in a search box send the request - and the
   search row is a sibling of <DtMap> rather than a child, because Leaflet owns that container's DOM
   and anything rendered inside it is wiped on the first pan. *@
@if (_offersRequest)
{
    <DtDialog @ref="_requestDialog" Title="@Localizer["RequestDeliveryTitle"]" OnClosed="ClearFailures">
        <ChildContent>
            @* Inside the dialog, deliberately. A DtDialog opens with showModal(), so a refusal
               rendered at page level would sit behind the backdrop and say nothing. It keeps
               whatever the boxes below have not claimed - a 403 or a 409 names no field. *@
            <DtFailureBanner Failures="_failures" InlineFields="@InlineFields" />

            <div class="mb-3">
                <label class="form-label" for="request-pickup-search">@Localizer["AddressSearch"]</label>
                <div class="input-group">
                    <input id="request-pickup-search" class="form-control"
                           @bind="_request.PickupSearch.Query"
                           @bind:event="oninput"
                           @onkeydown="Enter(_request.PickupSearch)" />
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary dt-request-pickup-search"
                            disabled="@_request.PickupSearch.IsBusy"
                            @onclick="Search(_request.PickupSearch)">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.Search" />@Localizer["AddressSearchAction"]
                    </button>
                </div>
                <div class="form-text">@Localizer["AddressSearchHint"]</div>
                @* The box's own banner, under the box it is about (DW-41). The dialog's banner above
                   belongs to the request itself, and a search that writes into it is a search that
                   can erase a refusal nobody has read. *@
                <DtFailureBanner Failures="_request.PickupSearch.Failures" />
                @if (_request.PickupSearch.FoundNothing)
                {
                    <div class="form-text dt-request-pickup-empty" role="status">@Localizer["AddressSearchEmpty"]</div>
                }
                @if (_request.PickupSearch.Matches.Count > 0)
                {
                    <ul class="list-group mb-2">
                        @foreach (var match in _request.PickupSearch.Matches)
                        {
                            <li class="list-group-item">
                                <button type="button" class="btn btn-link dt-request-pickup-match"
                                        @onclick="Choose(_request.PickupSearch, match)">@match.Address</button>
                            </li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                }
                @* No `for` on the map's label: a map is a region rather than a labelable control, and
                   DtMap carries its own aria-label. The search box above it is an input and has one. *@
                <label class="form-label">@Localizer["Pickup"]</label>
                <DtMap Value="_request.Pickup" ValueChanged="PickRequestPickup" Picker="true" />
                @* Under the map rather than in the dialog's banner: an unpicked point is refused
                   with COMMON_FIELD_REQUIRED - "Обов’язкове поле" - which says nothing at all about
                   which control, and the map is half a screen tall. Beneath it, the sentence needs
                   nothing more. *@
                <DtFieldError Field="Pickup" Failures="_failures" />
            </div>
            <div class="mb-3">
                <label class="form-label" for="request-dropoff-search">@Localizer["AddressSearch"]</label>
                <div class="input-group">
                    <input id="request-dropoff-search" class="form-control"
                           @bind="_request.DropoffSearch.Query"
                           @bind:event="oninput"
                           @onkeydown="Enter(_request.DropoffSearch)" />
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary dt-request-dropoff-search"
                            disabled="@_request.DropoffSearch.IsBusy"
                            @onclick="Search(_request.DropoffSearch)">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.Search" />@Localizer["AddressSearchAction"]
                    </button>
                </div>
                <div class="form-text">@Localizer["AddressSearchHint"]</div>
                @* See the pickup box above. *@
                <DtFailureBanner Failures="_request.DropoffSearch.Failures" />
                @if (_request.DropoffSearch.FoundNothing)
                {
                    <div class="form-text dt-request-dropoff-empty" role="status">@Localizer["AddressSearchEmpty"]</div>
                }
                @if (_request.DropoffSearch.Matches.Count > 0)
                {
                    <ul class="list-group mb-2">
                        @foreach (var match in _request.DropoffSearch.Matches)
                        {
                            <li class="list-group-item">
                                <button type="button" class="btn btn-link dt-request-dropoff-match"
                                        @onclick="Choose(_request.DropoffSearch, match)">@match.Address</button>
                            </li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                }
                <label class="form-label">@Localizer["Dropoff"]</label>
                <DtMap Value="_request.Dropoff" ValueChanged="PickRequestDropoff" Picker="true" />
                <DtFieldError Field="Dropoff" Failures="_failures" />
            </div>

            <EditForm id="request-form" Model="_request" OnValidSubmit="SubmitRequestAsync">
                @* Clearing the number field leaves a parse failure on the EditContext and
                   OnValidSubmit then never fires, so without this the button would do nothing and
                   say nothing. ValidationSummary rather than per-field ValidationMessage: a
                   `For="@(() => ...)"` lambda inside an attribute is what NFR-14's scan misreads. *@
                <ValidationSummary />

                <div class="mb-3">
                    <label class="form-label" for="request-details">@Localizer["PackageDetails"]</label>
                    <InputText id="request-details" class="form-control" @bind-Value="_request.PackageDetails" />
                    <DtFieldError Field="PackageDetails" Failures="_failures" />
                </div>
                <div class="mb-3">
                    <label class="form-label" for="request-weight">@Localizer["PackageWeightKg"]</label>
                    @* No {0} in the message: the framework fills it with the CLR property name,
                       which would put a Latin word in front of a user (NFR-14). *@
                    <InputNumber id="request-weight" class="form-control"
                                 ParsingErrorMessage="@Localizer["NumberParseError"]"
                                 @bind-Value="_request.PackageWeightKg" />
                    @* No capacity hint here, and none is missing: a request names no driver, so
                       there is no vehicle whose capacity a client could be about to exceed. *@
                    <DtFieldError Field="PackageWeightKg" Failures="_failures" />
                </div>
                <div class="mb-3">
                    <label class="form-label" for="request-notes">@Localizer["DeliveryNotes"]</label>
                    <InputTextArea id="request-notes" class="form-control" @bind-Value="_request.DeliveryNotes" />
                    <DtFieldError Field="DeliveryNotes" Failures="_failures" />
                </div>
            </EditForm>
        </ChildContent>
        <Actions>
            @* Disabled while a request is in flight: the circuit yields at the await, so a second
               click would ask for a second, identical Pending delivery, and there is no dedupe
               anywhere downstream to undo one that got through. *@
            <button type="submit" form="request-form" class="btn btn-primary dt-request-submit"
                    disabled="@_saving">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Confirm" />@Localizer["RequestDeliverySubmit"]
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="CloseRequestAsync">
                <Icon Name="IconName.Close" />@Localizer["Cancel"]
            </button>
        </Actions>
    </DtDialog>
}

@code {

    /// <summary>
    /// Drops the refusals when the form is dismissed, however it was dismissed.
    /// <para>
    /// FR-83. A refusal claimed by a box inside the dialog is subtracted from the banner outside it,
    /// so leaving the list populated past the close would leave that sentence with nowhere on the
    /// page to render - invisible, which is the outcome this whole change exists to prevent.
    /// Hung off <c>OnClosed</c> rather than off the cancel handler because Escape and the backdrop
    /// dismiss the dialog too, and the component routes all three through one event.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ClearFailures() => _failures = [];
    /// <summary>
    /// The boxes this screen renders a <c>DtFieldError</c> for. Stated once and bound by every
    /// banner on the page: the list repeated per banner is exactly the drift FR-83 is broken by,
    /// and a screen carries the banner more than once because a dialog opened with showModal()
    /// leaves the page behind it inert.
    /// </summary>
    private const string InlineFields = "Pickup,Dropoff,PackageDetails,PackageWeightKg,DeliveryNotes";

    private IReadOnlyList<AssignedDeliverySummary> _deliveries = [];

    /// <summary>The row whose history is open, or null while none is.</summary>
    private AssignedDeliverySummary? _timeline;

    /// <summary>The row whose capture panel is open, or null while none is.</summary>
    private AssignedDeliverySummary? _capturing;

    /// <summary>The row whose proof is open, or null while none is.</summary>
    private AssignedDeliverySummary? _viewing;

    /// <summary>
    /// The deliveries this screen knows carry a proof, so the capture action is withdrawn once one
    /// has been taken.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// It starts empty and grows, and that is honest rather than complete: the row shape carries no
    /// proof flag, and asking the capability once per row would be a hundred reads to draw a page.
    /// So the button is offered until this screen has reason to believe otherwise, and the
    /// authoritative refusal is the capability's own - a second capture is a 409 the panel shows
    /// (FR-123), not a button that was never drawn.
    /// </remarks>
    private readonly HashSet<int> _captured = [];

    private DtDialog? _mapDialog;
    private DtDialog? _timelineDialog;
    private DtDialog? _captureDialog;
    private DtDialog? _proofDialog;

    /// <summary>The point the map dialog is showing, or null before one has been chosen.</summary>
    private MapLocation? _viewed;

    /// <summary>The request panel, or null for a driver, whose page never renders one.</summary>
    private DtDialog? _requestDialog;

    /// <summary>What the request dialog is holding. Replaced on each open rather than cleared.</summary>
    private RequestForm _request = new();

    /// <summary>
    /// Why the last request submission was refused, rendered inside the request dialog. Not the
    /// address boxes' — each of those keeps its own (DW-41), so a search that succeeds cannot clear
    /// a refusal still on screen.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> _failures = [];

    private bool _loading = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// True while a request is in flight, which is what disables the submit button for as long as
    /// it lasts. A delivery carries no natural key the way a vehicle carries a licence plate, so
    /// nothing downstream would recognise the second click's row as a repeat of the first: the
    /// client would simply have asked for two identical Pending deliveries.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _saving;

    /// <summary>Whether this caller is offered the transition buttons.</summary>
    private bool _offersStatusChange;

    /// <summary>Whether this caller is offered the request action and the form behind it.</summary>
    private bool _offersRequest;

    /// <summary>The signed-in caller's role, read once the authenticated check below has passed.</summary>
    private UserRole _role;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a role has any lifecycle action on a delivery of their own. A client never does
    /// (FR-90), so offering them a button that could only ever be refused would be worse than
    /// offering none.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Internal and static so it can be asserted directly, for the reason the dispatch board lifts
    /// its filter and sort rules out: static rendering builds no panel until a row is chosen and
    /// dispatches no event that could choose one, so the flag's effect is unreachable from a render
    /// test — and inverting it would leave every suite green while a client was handed the buttons
    /// FR-90 says must not be drawn.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="role">The signed-in caller's role.</param>
    internal static bool OffersStatusChange(UserRole role) => role == UserRole.Driver;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this caller is offered the capture action on this row: the driver carrying the
    /// parcel, and only while the delivery has no proof (FR-119, FR-123).
    /// <para>
    /// A client is never offered it. FR-122 lets them read the evidence and nothing lets them make
    /// it, so a button that could only ever be refused would be worse than no button — and
    /// <c>IAccessGuard.RequireAssignedDriver</c> refuses their capture either way (FR-12).
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Internal and static so it can be asserted directly, for the reason
    /// <see cref="OffersStatusChange"/> is: static rendering dispatches no event that could capture
    /// anything, so the flag's effect is unreachable from a render test — and inverting it would
    /// leave every suite green while a client was handed an action FR-90's sibling rule says is not
    /// theirs.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="role">The signed-in caller's role.</param>
    /// <param name="hasProof">Whether this screen knows the delivery already carries a proof.</param>
    internal static bool OffersProofCapture(UserRole role, bool hasProof) =>
        role == UserRole.Driver && !hasProof;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this caller may ask for a delivery of their own (FR-89). A client may; a driver
    /// carries parcels and composes none, so they are offered neither the action nor the form.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Internal and static for the reason <see cref="OffersStatusChange"/> is, and with one
    /// difference that matters more here: this flag also decides whether the dialog is rendered at
    /// all, so inverting it would put a request form on a driver's page — markup
    /// <c>IAccessGuard.RequireDeliveryComposer</c> would refuse the moment it was used, but markup
    /// that should never have been drawn.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="role">The signed-in caller's role.</param>
    internal static bool OffersRequest(UserRole role) => role == UserRole.Client;

    /// <summary>
    /// True when the page came back full. The query returns no total (PRD section 8 excludes one),
    /// so "there may be more" is the honest claim rather than a count.
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsTruncated => _deliveries.Count >= ListDeliveriesQueryValidator.MaximumLimit;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // The circuit outlives the request that opened it, so the cookie can be gone by the time a
        // screen calls a service. Raising the contract's own code here is what puts that case
        // through FR-13's single boundary instead of a per-screen redirect.
        if (!Caller.IsAuthenticated)
        {
            throw new ForbiddenException(
                ErrorCode.AUTH_UNAUTHENTICATED,
                "The circuit has no authenticated caller: the session cookie is gone.");
        }

        // Read after the authenticated check: ICurrentUser.Role throws for an anonymous caller, so
        // asking outside that branch would take the screen down instead of refusing it.
        _role = Caller.Role;
        _offersStatusChange = OffersStatusChange(_role);
        _offersRequest = OffersRequest(_role);

        await LoadAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Opens the request panel on a form with nothing in it, so a refused attempt that was closed
    /// and reopened does not come back holding the values that were refused.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OpenRequestAsync()
    {
        _request = new RequestForm();
        _failures = [];

        if (_requestDialog is not null)
        {
            await _requestDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task CloseRequestAsync()
    {
        if (_requestDialog is not null)
        {
            await _requestDialog.CloseAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Sends the request (FR-89 to FR-91) and refreshes the list it will appear in.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The command carries five values and nothing else. There is no driver to send, no status to
    /// send and no client id to send: the row is attached to whoever asked, which the guard decides
    /// inside the capability and this screen never names.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task SubmitRequestAsync()
    {
        if (_saving)
        {
            return;
        }

        // Set before the await and cleared in the finally: the button is disabled from the moment
        // the first click is handled until the server has answered, so a second click has nothing
        // to act on.
        _saving = true;

        try
        {
            await Mine.RequestAsync(_request.ToCommand(), CircuitToken);

            _failures = [];

            await CloseRequestAsync();
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return;
        }
        finally
        {
            _saving = false;
        }

        // Outside the try, deliberately. The request has committed, so a failure refreshing the
        // list afterwards is a failed read, and reporting it as a refused request would tell the
        // client their delivery was not asked for when it was.
        await LoadAsync();
    }

    private void PickRequestPickup(MapLocation picked) => _request.Pickup = picked;

    private void PickRequestDropoff(MapLocation picked) => _request.Dropoff = picked;

    /// <summary>
    /// One box's search action, bound as a delegate so no lambda has to live in markup — a <c>=&gt;</c>
    /// in an attribute closes the tag as far as NFR-14's markup scan is concerned.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The box is a parameter rather than one method per point, so the markup names the same holder
    /// it rendered the list from and there is no second place the pickup handler could be wired to
    /// the dropoff box. The three-character floor is the capability's and is deliberately not
    /// repeated here; the hint under the box is what tells the client about it beforehand.
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing is assigned here, and that is DW-41: the box keeps its own failures, so a search that
    /// succeeds cannot clear a submit refusal the client has not read - nor the other box's.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="search">The box being searched with.</param>
    private Func<Task> Search(PlaceSearch search) =>
        () => search.RunAsync(Mine.SearchPlacesAsync, CircuitToken);

    /// <summary>
    /// The same action on Enter, which is the gesture a search box invites. Anything else typed
    /// falls through and is ignored.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// No <c>preventDefault</c> to go with it, and none is needed: the two boxes are rendered
    /// outside <c>EditForm</c> precisely so Enter has no form to submit. The busy check is the same
    /// guard the button carries as <c>disabled</c> — Enter is a key that can be held down, and
    /// without it a held key is a burst of requests to a public service.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="search">The box being searched with.</param>
    private Func<KeyboardEventArgs, Task> Enter(PlaceSearch search) => async args =>
    {
        if (args.Key == "Enter" && !search.IsBusy)
        {
            await search.RunAsync(Mine.SearchPlacesAsync, CircuitToken);
        }
    };

    /// <summary>A match's selection action, for the reason <see cref="Search" /> takes its box.</summary>
    /// <param name="search">The box the match came from.</param>
    /// <param name="match">The match chosen.</param>
    private static Action Choose(PlaceSearch search, PlaceMatch match) => () => search.Choose(match);

    /// <summary>The location cell's action, bound as a delegate for the same reason.</summary>
    private Func<Task> ShowLocationHandler(LocationView location) =>
        () => ShowLocationAsync(location);

    private async Task ShowLocationAsync(LocationView location)
    {
        _viewed = location.Point;

        if (_mapDialog is not null)
        {
            await _mapDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The row's history action, bound as a delegate so no lambda has to live in markup.</summary>
    private Func<Task> TimelineHandler(AssignedDeliverySummary delivery) =>
        () => ShowTimelineAsync(delivery);

    private async Task ShowTimelineAsync(AssignedDeliverySummary delivery)
    {
        _timeline = delivery;

        if (_timelineDialog is not null)
        {
            await _timelineDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Forgets which row the history dialog was showing, so the next open builds a new panel rather
    /// than reusing one holding a stale status and a half-typed note.
    /// </summary>
    private void CloseTimeline() => _timeline = null;

    /// <summary>The row's capture action, bound as a delegate so no lambda has to live in markup.</summary>
    private Func<Task> CaptureHandler(AssignedDeliverySummary delivery) =>
        () => ShowCaptureAsync(delivery);

    /// <inheritdoc cref="CaptureHandler" />
    private Func<Task> ProofHandler(AssignedDeliverySummary delivery) =>
        () => ShowProofAsync(delivery);

    private async Task ShowCaptureAsync(AssignedDeliverySummary delivery)
    {
        _capturing = delivery;

        if (_captureDialog is not null)
        {
            await _captureDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task ShowProofAsync(AssignedDeliverySummary delivery)
    {
        _viewing = delivery;

        if (_proofDialog is not null)
        {
            await _proofDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Closes the capture dialog once a proof has committed and withdraws the action for that row.
    /// The list is refetched too: FR-120 means the delivery can now be marked delivered with no
    /// note, and the timeline panel behind this one reads its state from the same load.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task CapturedAsync()
    {
        if (_capturing is { } delivery)
        {
            _captured.Add(delivery.Id);
        }

        if (_captureDialog is not null)
        {
            await _captureDialog.CloseAsync();
        }

        await LoadAsync();
    }

    private void CloseCapture() => _capturing = null;

    private void CloseProof() => _viewing = null;

    private async Task LoadAsync()
    {
        _loading = true;

        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            // The caller says nothing about whose rows it wants: the narrowing comes from the
            // guard's scope and reaches the SQL, so the first page really is the first page of
            // this caller's deliveries rather than the first page of everyone's, filtered.
            _deliveries = await Mine.ListMineAsync(
                new ListDeliveriesQuery(0, ListDeliveriesQueryValidator.MaximumLimit),
                CircuitToken);
        }
        finally
        {
            // In a finally so a refused or failed read leaves the table in its empty state rather
            // than spinning forever, which reads to a user as a hung screen.
            _loading = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The location as the cell shows it, shared with the dispatch board (FR-94).</summary>
    private static string Describe(LocationView location) => LocationText.Describe(location);

    /// <summary>
    /// The latest acceptable arrival in local time, or nothing when the delivery carries no window.
    /// Routed through a method rather than formatted in markup: NFR-14's scan strips a Razor
    /// expression only up to its first closing bracket.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Local is the server's configured zone, as it is on the dispatch board: the shell renders
    /// interactively on the server, so <c>ToLocalTime</c> resolves against the host.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Window(AssignedDeliverySummary delivery) =>
        delivery.WindowLatestAt is { } latest
            ? latest.ToLocalTime().ToString("g", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)
            : string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the request dialog is holding: exactly the five values
    /// <see cref="RequestDeliveryCommand" /> carries, plus the two address boxes that fill two of
    /// them.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Internal for the reason the dispatch board's form is: static rendering dispatches no events,
    /// so nothing a render test can do reaches these two boxes, and the wiring between a box and the
    /// point it fills would otherwise be asserted nowhere at all. The pair is constructed here
    /// rather than assigned from outside, so "the dropoff box sets the dropoff" stays one readable
    /// line instead of two handlers on the screen that could be crossed.
    /// <para>
    /// There is no driver, client, status or window property, and that is the story rather than an
    /// omission: the command has no field for one, so a form that carried one would be collecting a
    /// value with nowhere to send it.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    internal sealed class RequestForm
    {
        /// <summary>A form with two empty address boxes, each bound to its own point.</summary>
        public RequestForm()
        {
            PickupSearch = new PlaceSearch(point => Pickup = point);
            DropoffSearch = new PlaceSearch(point => Dropoff = point);
        }

        public MapLocation? Pickup { get; set; }

        public MapLocation? Dropoff { get; set; }

        /// <summary>FR-104's box above the pickup map, wired to <em>this</em> form's pickup point.</summary>
        public PlaceSearch PickupSearch { get; }

        /// <inheritdoc cref="PickupSearch" />
        public PlaceSearch DropoffSearch { get; }

        public string? PackageDetails { get; set; }

        public decimal PackageWeightKg { get; set; }

        public string? DeliveryNotes { get; set; }

        /// <summary>What this form sends, as the five values the command carries.</summary>
        /// <remarks>
        /// Here rather than inline in <c>SubmitRequestAsync</c> for the reason the boxes are
        /// constructed here: the two points are the pair that can be crossed, and a screen method
        /// building the command is a crossing no test in a static-rendering harness can reach.
        /// Built here, it is a plain object a test can construct and read back.
        /// </remarks>
        public RequestDeliveryCommand ToCommand() =>
            new(
                Coordinates(Pickup),
                Coordinates(Dropoff),
                PackageDetails,
                PackageWeightKg,
                DeliveryNotes);

        /// <summary>
        /// The coordinates a picked point sends, or null when nothing has been picked — which the
        /// capability refuses with a field name rather than this screen guessing at one.
        /// </summary>
        private static LocationInput? Coordinates(MapLocation? point) =>
            point is { } value ? new LocationInput(value.Latitude, value.Longitude) : null;
    }
}
